  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1244 PM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nOver the next few days, a deepening coastal low along the \nnortheastern seaboard on the eastern flank of an upper-level \nridge over Upper Midwest will reinforce a Rex-like blocking \npattern across the Great Lakes. As a result, relatively \nquiescent conditions will continue across our area through into\nearly next week with no weather concerns of note. Highs will \nrange from the upper 60s to lower 70s, lows will fall to the \nupper 40s to lower 50s, and winds will remain fairly light (10 \nmph or less) out of the east. Upper-level shortwaves will meet \ntheir demise as they propagate toward the blocked upper- level \npattern, leading to occasional periods of mid to upper- level \ncloud cover and perhaps a few sprinkles particularly through \nSunday morning.\n\nToward the middle of next week, a series of waves originating \nfrom the northern Pacific region will lead to the development of\na more progressive pattern across the CONUS. Ensemble \nprobabilities for soaking rain increase incrementally across \nmuch of the central and Midwestern US by the middle of the week\nas each wave propagates overhead. With that said, will note \nless clarity on exact details (favored time windows, rainfall \namounts, opportunity for thunder) owing to spread on the \nevolution, speed, and arrival time of each wave. Whether or not \nthe remnants of Pacific hurricane Polo influences the large- \nscale pattern remains an item of interest, as well. Blended NBM \nguidance offers broad-brushed change to likely (30-60%) PoPs \nfrom Wednesday into the first few days of October, which seems \nfine for the time being. \n\nBorchardt",
